WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.480
A college tuition that runs $30,000 a year rests on instruction whose direct delivery cost

00:00:05.480 --> 00:00:12.420
sits at a few hundred dollars per student. This is First Principles Daily, episode 72. A

00:00:12.420 --> 00:00:18.320
four-year degree at many private colleges now carries a sticker price above $120,000.

00:00:19.480 --> 00:00:24.340
The classroom time, basic facilities, and digital materials that actually convey the subject

00:00:24.340 --> 00:00:30.460
matter could be assembled for a few hundred dollars per student in a large scale setting. The difference

00:00:30.460 --> 00:00:35.740
between those two figures is not explained by the physics of teaching. It is explained by the

00:00:35.740 --> 00:00:41.960
separate market for credentials that employers and regulators treat as non-negotiable. Universities

00:00:41.960 --> 00:00:48.380
price degrees primarily as signals rather than as bundles of instructional hours. Employers use

00:00:48.380 --> 00:00:54.700
the diploma as a filter because verifying individual competence is expensive at scale, so the credential

00:00:54.700 --> 00:01:00.960
itself carries the value. A accreditation body requires specific faculty credentials, credit

00:01:00.960 --> 00:01:07.120
our minimums, and physical or administrative infrastructure that adds fixed costs unrelated to learning

00:01:07.120 --> 00:01:13.240
outcomes. Federal loan guarantees reduce price sensitivity for students. This allows sticker

00:01:13.240 --> 00:01:19.560
prices to rise without immediate demand destruction. State licensing rules for many professions tie

00:01:19.560 --> 00:01:25.520
eligibility to accredited degrees. That further insulates the price from competition by unbundled

00:01:25.520 --> 00:01:31.300
providers. On-campus construction and maintenance follow legacy standards for residential colleges

00:01:31.300 --> 00:01:36.940
that date to errors when most students lived on site. Administrative overhead has grown

00:01:36.940 --> 00:01:42.380
to manage compliance, financial aid, and student services that are now expected as part of

00:01:42.380 --> 00:01:48.840
the package. Research universities cross subsidize labs and graduate programs with undergraduate tuition.

00:01:49.540 --> 00:01:55.480
They spread those costs across all students. Legacy assumptions about four-year residential timelines

00:01:55.480 --> 00:02:02.140
persist, even though many students attend part-time or online. Custom advising, small seminars,

00:02:02.440 --> 00:02:07.920
and extracurricular facilities remain priced into the average even when most students use only a

00:02:07.920 --> 00:02:14.320
subset. The result is that the published price feels normal inside the industry. Every participant

00:02:14.320 --> 00:02:19.520
optimizes for the credential market rather than the marginal cost of adding one more student to

00:02:19.520 --> 00:02:26.200
an existing course. Each of these layers reinforces the others. An employer who accepts only the accredited

00:02:26.200 --> 00:02:32.340
diploma creates demand for the full residential package. That in turn justifies higher administrative

00:02:32.340 --> 00:02:37.760
staffing to handle the resulting compliance workload. When state boards list an accredited

00:02:37.760 --> 00:02:43.380
degree as the sole entry route for a profession, alternative instructional models lose the ability

00:02:43.380 --> 00:02:49.520
to compete on price alone. Loan programs that disperse funds to institutions rather than directly

00:02:49.520 --> 00:02:55.760
to demonstrated outcomes further lock the revenue model in place. Over time, these interlocking

00:02:55.760 --> 00:03:01.820
requirements turn the credential into the dominant product. Instruction becomes a secondary input whose

00:03:01.820 --> 00:03:08.500
marginal cost is rarely examined. A rough estimate of the raw delivery floor begins with faculty time.

00:03:09.140 --> 00:03:15.380
An instructor paid roughly $80,000 a year who teaches 200 students per term across two terms

00:03:15.380 --> 00:03:22.020
produces a labor cost near $200 per student per term before benefits or preparation time.

00:03:22.740 --> 00:03:28.400
Classroom space amortized across hundreds of students and multiple daily uses adds perhaps

00:03:28.400 --> 00:03:33.860
another $50 per student per term when measured against commercial real estate rates rather than

00:03:33.860 --> 00:03:39.540
university construction standards. Digital platforms and recorded materials, once created,

00:03:39.960 --> 00:03:46.120
carry near zero marginal cost per additional viewer. Adding basic utilities, basic connectivity,

00:03:46.620 --> 00:03:52.440
and modest administrative coordination brings a plausible magic one total for one term of instruction

00:03:52.440 --> 00:03:58.500
into the low hundreds of dollars. Published tuition and fees at many four-year institutions run between

00:03:58.500 --> 00:04:06.080
$10,000 and $40,000 per year. Dividing those figures produces an idiot index of roughly 50 to 200.

00:04:06.620 --> 00:04:12.620
The gap lives in several distinct layers. Accreditation and regulatory compliance require dedicated

00:04:12.620 --> 00:04:19.340
staff and documentation processes that do not scale with enrollment. Federal loan administration and

00:04:19.340 --> 00:04:24.280
institutional financial aid offices add overhead whose cost is recovered through tuition.

00:04:25.040 --> 00:04:30.480
The signaling premium itself is not a physical input. It is the market price of the document

00:04:30.480 --> 00:04:35.980
that employers accept in place of direct assessment. Residential facilities, athletics,

00:04:36.300 --> 00:04:40.960
and student life programming are priced into the average even for students who do not use them.

00:04:41.660 --> 00:04:45.680
Each of these layers compounds because they are funded by the same revenue stream.

00:04:46.240 --> 00:04:50.060
The credential remains the scarce good that students are ultimately purchasing.

00:04:50.900 --> 00:04:56.440
Consider the compliance layer more closely. Maintaining accreditation involves annual reports,

00:04:56.980 --> 00:05:02.180
site visits, and curriculum mapping that consume full-time staff hours unrelated to the number

00:05:02.180 --> 00:05:08.120
of students actually enrolled in any given course. Those fixed hours must still be paid from tuition

00:05:08.120 --> 00:05:14.300
revenue, so the per student share rises whenever enrollment growth lags behind administrative expansion.

00:05:15.020 --> 00:05:20.420
The same dynamic applies to financial aid offices that process forms required by federal rules

00:05:20.420 --> 00:05:25.660
even when a student's actual instructional load is delivered through low-cost digital channels.

00:05:26.060 --> 00:05:30.720
Because these offices exist to unlock the loan dollars that support the sticker price,

00:05:31.300 --> 00:05:36.960
their costs become self-reinforcing. The signaling layer adds still more distance from the floor.

00:05:37.620 --> 00:05:43.440
Once employers treat the diploma as a binary screen, any provider that offers only the instructional

00:05:43.440 --> 00:05:48.440
component must overcome the risk that its graduates will be filtered out before their competence is

00:05:48.440 --> 00:05:54.000
ever tested. A redesign would first separate the instructional product from the credential product.

00:05:54.740 --> 00:06:00.080
Standardized, high-quality, recorded, and interactive courseware could be produced once and distributed

00:06:00.080 --> 00:06:06.440
at marginal cost near zero, once enrollment reaches tens of thousands. Employers or independent

00:06:06.440 --> 00:06:11.880
testing bodies would then need to accept proctored assessments or work sample portfolios in place of

00:06:11.880 --> 00:06:17.020
institutional degrees. That shift requires changes in hiring practices and licensing

00:06:17.020 --> 00:06:23.100
statutes that currently name accredited degrees as the sole qualifying path. Scale would have to

00:06:23.100 --> 00:06:29.280
be large enough to amortize content production across many cohorts. That in turn demands reliable

00:06:29.280 --> 00:06:35.740
demand from employers willing to hire undemonstrated competence. Policy changes would be needed to let

00:06:35.740 --> 00:06:41.940
federal aid follow students to non-accredited providers that meet transparent outcome standards rather

00:06:41.940 --> 00:06:48.280
than input requirements. The hardest constraints are not technical. Employers must overcome the

00:06:48.280 --> 00:06:54.420
coordination problem of moving away from a widely accepted signal. Regulators must rewrite rules

00:06:54.420 --> 00:07:00.860
that treat seat time and institutional accreditation as proxies for quality. Students must accept

00:07:00.880 --> 00:07:05.780
credentials whose market value is initially uncertain while the new system proves itself.

00:07:06.500 --> 00:07:10.620
Those frictions explain why the price has remained far above the delivery floor,

00:07:11.060 --> 00:07:16.600
even though the underlying materials and labor have been available for decades. A second concrete

00:07:16.600 --> 00:07:21.960
move would involve modular assessment centers that operate independently of any single institution.

00:07:22.580 --> 00:07:27.120
They would allow multiple courseware providers to feed into the same verification pipeline.

00:07:27.840 --> 00:07:32.860
Such centers would need to demonstrate consistent reliability across thousands of test takers

00:07:32.860 --> 00:07:38.900
before employers would rely on their output. A third move would target the residential subsidy.

00:07:39.500 --> 00:07:44.860
If the instructional component can be priced separately, facilities and student life costs could

00:07:44.860 --> 00:07:49.820
be billed only to those who use them rather than averaged across every tuition pair.

00:07:50.580 --> 00:07:56.220
Each of these steps still collides with the same coordination barrier. Until a critical mass of

00:07:56.220 --> 00:08:02.380
employers and regulators treat the new verification path as equivalent, the old bundled price remains

00:08:02.380 --> 00:08:08.400
the safer choice for students. When the largest share of a price is attached to verification rather than

00:08:08.400 --> 00:08:15.240
production, the opportunity lies in rebuilding the verification step itself. When regulation and signaling

00:08:15.240 --> 00:08:21.940
both treat an old format as the only acceptable proof, the cost stays anchored to that format regardless

00:08:21.940 --> 00:08:27.540
of how cheaply the underlying service can be delivered. The first concrete signal that someone

00:08:27.540 --> 00:08:33.020
is attempting this would be a large employer publicly committing to higher cohorts based on standardized

00:08:33.020 --> 00:08:39.060
assessments, rather than degree status. That would be followed by measurable changes in recruiting

00:08:39.060 --> 00:08:44.960
pipelines. A second signal would appear when licensing boards publish explicit pathways for

00:08:44.960 --> 00:08:51.020
non-degree candidates to demonstrate equivalent competence through portfolios or third-party exams.

00:08:51.740 --> 00:08:57.160
Both signals would indicate that the coordination problem is beginning to loosen. They would show that

00:08:57.160 --> 00:09:03.000
the gap between delivery cost and credential price is starting to narrow. That's a wrap on today's

00:09:03.000 --> 00:09:08.840
first principles breakdown. The ideas travel further when you pass them on. Send this to a fellow

00:09:08.840 --> 00:09:14.320
builder. I'm Patrick and Vancouver. See you tomorrow. And if you'd rather watch then listen,

